WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:05.890
Peter Grant: Well, over the last 40 years,
since the time we started our Galapagos research

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:11.710
there have been two or three major changes
in general understanding of how evolution

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:13.780
works.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:19.580
The first is a big development in computer
technology which has allowed people to analyze

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:21.560
large quantities of data.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:28.119
And the second one is a very large development
in molecular genetics, which has given many

00:00:28.119 --> 00:00:32.340
biologists the tools to examining evolution
at genetic level.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:38.750
And the third one is the field studies that
have been done that allow people to understand

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:42.430
just how rapidly evolution takes place.

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:49.990
Not over centuries, not over decades, but
over a short period of time such as a year

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:51.720
for organisms as large as birds.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.860
Rosemary Grant: The general theory of evolution
has changed very little.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:03.030
What actually has changed are the techniques
that allowed us to study it.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:06.700
And one of the biggest ones is the genetic
techniques.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:15.060
So now that we are able to sequence whole
genomes, we've been able to look at the genes

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:18.580
that are responsible for some of the changes
that we have seen in the field.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.500
So this has been been extremely exciting.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:28.890
And so it is more that we are able to go in
depth to a much greater degree than they were

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:29.390
before.

